# Barber application examples

## Ownership and dependencies

- `examples/barber/mongodb/`, `postgres/`, and `frontend/` are private npm apps with their own manifests and lockfiles. Run `npm ci` inside the affected app; root workspace installation does not install them.
- Keep the apps outside `packages/*` workspaces and library release/version scripts. Backend Simfinity runtime packages use exact released version `3.8.0` from npm. Do not restore local vendor archives or file dependencies.
- The frontend source is shared. Its GraphQL endpoint is selected by the environment at build time and must be reachable by the browser. Treat IDs as opaque GraphQL `ID` strings: MongoDB uses ObjectIds and PostgreSQL uses UUIDs.
- Scopes must intersect caller filters with server restrictions. Use `intersectScopeFilter` instead of overwriting an existing scalar, relation, ID or OR filter.
- Scopes run once per parent row and per resolved reference. Express restrictions on related records as relation-path terms (`ownedBarbershopTerm`/`approvedBarbershopTerm` in `types/scopeHelpers.js`, e.g. `barbershop.state`, `barbershop.owner.id`) instead of loading ID lists inside a scope.
- Keep `auth/ownership.js` and the booking, bundle, service and review controllers mirrored in both backends. `protect()` counts a field as written only when it differs from the stored value (`writtenFields`, `clears`), and refuses a non-admin delete of a shop that bookings or reviews reference, or of a booking that a review references, with `RESOURCE_IN_USE` (409) on both backends. Derive booking and bundle values (line prices and durations, totals, `endTime`, confirmation codes, creation times) on the server from the shop's catalog; never trust client-sent values. A service's duration change or delete refreshes the stored `totalDurationMinutes` of the bundles that list it (`refreshBundleDurationsForService`), and the frontend derives a bundle's duration with the same rule (`src/lib/catalog.ts`).
- Preserve the same domain concepts, relationship metadata, scopes, controller responsibilities, and state transitions. Native Mongoose and PostgreSQL model/session calls remain separate. Choose the backend at startup; do not add runtime switching or automatic data migration.

## Operation and data

- Run Compose from `examples/barber` with `-f compose.mongodb.yaml` or `-f compose.postgres.yaml`. Retain the distinct project names `simfinity-example-barber-mongodb` and `simfinity-example-barber-postgres`, named volumes, and loopback bindings so both stacks can coexist.
- Default host ports are MongoDB `57417`, API `4400`, frontend `4401`; PostgreSQL `55441`, API `4500`, frontend `4501`. Backend/frontend container ports are `4300`/`4301`. Changes must update `.env.example` files, scripts, tests and READMEs together.
- Await database readiness before serving `/health`, `/graphql` and `/mcp`. HTTP, stdio, seeds and operational scripts should reuse each backend's application/database initialization and auth context.
- Request entry points read the account: GraphQL and uploads use `resolveUserContext` (an expired or invalid bearer, or a suspended or deleted account, is anonymous), HTTP `/mcp` and `mcp:stdio` use `resolveMcpUserContext` (it throws `UNAUTHENTICATED` or `ACCOUNT_SUSPENDED` for each of these; only a request without a bearer is anonymous). Both take the role from the database. `buildUserContext` is token-only; do not use it for requests.
- Mount the HTTP MCP handler with `app.all('/mcp', limiters.mcp, express.json(), mcpBodyErrors, rejectJsonRpcBatch, mcpHandler)` so non-POST methods get its `405` with `Allow: POST`, not an Express `404`, and unparsable bodies get JSON-RPC errors. Keep `jsonErrorHandler` (`routes/errors.js`) as the app's last middleware, so no error answers with Express's HTML page or a stack trace. In `useErrorHandler`, pass each entry of `errors` to the `buildErrorFormatter` logger; never pass the formatter itself, which then logs only `Unexpected error value`.
- `mcpOptions` in `application.js` is the single source of MCP options for `index.yoga.js`, `mcp:stdio` and the integration tests; entry points add only their `context`. Never publish the auth mutations (`AUTH_OPERATIONS`: `login`, `register`, `registerOwner`, `refreshSession`) as MCP tools, and reject JSON-RPC batches before the MCP handler.
- Keep the per-route limiters in `routes/rateLimits.js` (`graphql`, `mcp`, `uploadIp`, `upload`) and their environment names (`GRAPHQL_RATE_LIMIT_MAX`, `MCP_RATE_LIMIT_MAX`, `UPLOAD_IP_RATE_LIMIT_MAX`, `UPLOAD_RATE_LIMIT_MAX`). The per-account upload limiter runs after `requireAuth`.
- Uploads take their type and extension from the file's bytes (JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF; no SVG) and stage under `uploads/.incoming`, never in memory. They accept one `file` part and no other fields or parts; a Node system error while staging answers 500 without server paths. `createUploadRouter` sweeps abandoned staged files at startup, and shutdown awaits `router.settled()` after closing connections. `serveUploads` sends nosniff and the sandbox CSP on every file and serves non-raster files as `application/octet-stream` attachments, except legacy `.svg`, which keeps `image/svg+xml` (still an attachment) so `<img>` shows it.
- `seed:admin` precedes `seed:demo` and never promotes an existing non-admin account. The synthetic demo uses `barber-demo`, `admin@demo.com`, `propietario@demo.com`, `cliente@demo.com`, and password `demo1234`. Preserve repeatable seeding and browser-test compatibility.
- Reset only the selected example project. Data-changing scripts and tests must use disposable databases. `dataset:delete` deletes all records of its covered entity types at its endpoint, not only records bearing a fixture marker.
- Do not track real environment files, user uploads, dependency directories, generated frontend output, vendor archives, or PostgreSQL schema exports. `schema:export` writes current SDL, SQL and real catalog FK descriptions under ignored `postgres/generated/`.

## Verification and documentation

- `.github/workflows/barber.yml` owns backend unit tests, the database HTTP/MCP matrix, frontend checks and browser coverage, and database integration checks. Root ESLint and Vitest exclude examples.
- Each app exposes `npm run test:security` and its CI job audits its own complete lockfile, including development tools. Keep Vitest/browser/coverage versions compatible and do not bypass peer conflicts with `--force` or `--legacy-peer-deps`. Preserve the supported majors and exact released Simfinity dependencies when updating security baselines.
- `test:query-mutations` runs the shared HTTP filter/aggregate/nested-mutation matrix. Its missing-reference case explicitly distinguishes PostgreSQL FK rejection from MongoDB dangling-reference acceptance. It leaves synthetic fixtures in the disposable API database; CI saves its JSON reports.
- Run both database HTTP/browser paths for shared contracts. Preserve `test:mongodb` and both `test:dataset` suites for transactions, derived booking/bundle/review values and fixture operations. Keep PostgreSQL native model, FK, transaction and frontend-query regression checks. Do not use library unit tests as a substitute for app validation.
- Update `examples/barber/README.md`, the affected app README, and `docs/resources/barber.md` when setup or documented behavior changes. Preserve source-commit provenance and `frontend/LICENSE`.
- Describe the app as a synthetic local MVP. Its presence in the monorepo does not change the runtime library compatibility contract or require an npm library release.
